      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Perioperative opioids reduce inhalational anaesthetic requirements. The initial hypoalgesia may, however, be followed by a rebound hyperalgesia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To determine whether prior opioid administration influences inhalational anaesthetic requirements, which might be associated with opioid-induced hyperalgesia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "A prospective, randomised, experimental study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Experimental Surgery, La Paz University Hospital, Madrid, Spain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "ANIMALS",
          "content": "Seventy-nine adult male Wistar rats."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "Sevoflurane minimum alveolar concentration (MAC) and mechanical nociceptive thresholds (MNTs) were assessed at baseline and 7 days later following opioid treatment with remifentanil 120\u200aμg \u200akg-1 \u200ah-1, buprenorphine 150\u200aμg\u200akg-1, methadone 8\u200amg \u200akg-1 or morphine 10\u200amg \u200akg-1 The duration of the effect of remifentanil on MAC and MNT was evaluated in addition to the preventive effect of ketamine 10\u200amg \u200akg-1 on remifentanil-induced hyperalgesia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "The effect of different opioid treatments on MAC and MNT was evaluated using analysis of variance (ANOVA)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "All studied opioids produced an immediate reduction in sevoflurane MAC, followed by an increase (16%) in baseline MAC 7 days later (P\u200a<\u200a0.05), although the immediate MAC reduction produced by these opioids at that time was not different. Remifentanil produced a decrease in MNT (P\u200a<\u200a0.05), which was associated with an increase in the MAC (P\u200a<\u200a0.05) that persisted at 21 days. The effect of remifentanil on MNT and MAC was blocked by ketamine."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Opioid-induced hyperalgesia was associated with an increase in the MAC in normal rats who had not undergone surgery. Both effects lasted 21 days and were prevented by ketamine."
        }
      ]},
